    d: "Recognising patterns is the meta-skill that ties your problem practice together: looking at an unfamiliar problem and seeing which of the standard techniques — two pointers, sliding window, BFS/DFS, binary search, DP, greedy — it maps to. Clues live in the problem statement: constraints that suggest O(n) or O(log n), words like 'contiguous' pointing to windows, 'shortest' hinting at BFS, 'minimise the maximum' signalling binary search. You will practise reading problems quickly and building a mental decision tree. This skill is what makes the difference at interview time, where you have minutes, not hours, to choose an approach, and it is equally valuable in real work, where experienced engineers recognise that a new feature is 'just a cache problem' or 'a queue with retries'.",
